The system provides a uniform, modular, and architecture‐indepen‐
dent  system for the implementation of drivers to control various
parallel devices, and to use different parallel port  chip  sets.
In  order to write new drivers or port existing drivers, the sys‐
tem provides the following  facilities:  architecture‐independent
macros  or  functions to access parallel ports mechanism to allow
various devices to share the same parallel port  a  interface  to
access  the  individual  pins  a user interface named that allows
parallel port access from outside the kernel without  conflicting
with  kernel‐in drivers.  The system has been designed to support
the development of standard and non‐standard  software:  It  uses
standard  and  non‐standard  parallel port accesses.  Another ap‐
proach to the system is to port existing drivers.   Various  dri‐
vers  have  already  been  ported:  should let you port any other
software even from other operating systems that  provide  similar
services.  Parallel port chip set support is provided by The sys‐
tem provides functions and macros to request service from the in‐
cluding  reads,  writes,  setting  of  parameters,  and  bus  re‐
quests/releases.  detects chip set and capabilities and  sets  up
interrupt  handling.   It  makes methods available for use to the
system.  The logical parallel port model chosen for the system is
the AT parallel port model.  Consequently, for the implementation
of most of the services provided by will translate into  I/O  in‐
structions on actual registers.  However, other parallel port im‐
plementations  may  require more than one I/O instruction to do a
single logical register operation on  data,  status  and  control
virtual  registers.  The parallel port may operate in the follow‐
